cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Governance Concentration Beyond Token Allocation: An Institutional Design Analysis of DePIN and DeFi"
message: "If you use this code or data, please cite as follows."
type: article
authors:
  - family-names: "Zukowski"
    given-names: "Zach"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-3642-2450"
    email: "zach@tokenization.systems"
    affiliation: "Tokenization Systems"
abstract: >-
  A 40-protocol cross-section documents that initial token allocation design
  does not predict post-distribution governance concentration (r = 0.04,
  p = 0.84, N = 37). Protocols with generous community distributions exhibit
  concentration patterns similar to those with heavy insider allocations.
  Sector membership predicts concentration (DePIN more concentrated than
  DeFi, Mann-Whitney p = 0.020, Cohen&apos;s d = 0.94; robust across all 30
  leave-one-out iterations; permutation p = 0.003) after adjusting for
  protocol age, log fully diluted valuation, and insider allocation.
  Delegation universally amplifies governance concentration across nine
  of ten protocols in the voting-layer subsample (range 1.2x to 11.4x;
  mean 5.3x), with one structural exception (ENS at 0.39x); magnitude
  varies by institutional design within sector. The exclusion methodology
  identifies 133 address exclusions across 38 protocols (125 unique
  addresses; five recur across multiple protocols) controlled by
  protocols themselves that appear on holder lists but cannot vote;
  correcting for these changes affected protocols' HHI by up to 18x
  (RENDER at 17.9x; median 2.3x across 32 protocols with complete
  pre-exclusion and post-exclusion HHI logged).
keywords:
  - tokenomics
  - institutional design
  - governance concentration
  - DAO governance
  - DePIN
  - delegation amplification
  - Herfindahl-Hirschman Index
  - burn-rule exclusion
  - polycentric governance
  - exclusion methodology
